Given a triangle with vertices a, b, c and a point p, the function should return True exactly when p lies inside the triangle or on one of its three edges.

The standard method is to compute the three signed areas of the sub-triangles a-p-b, b-p-c and c-p-a and compare their signs. For p to lie inside the triangle or on an edge, all three sub-areas must have the same sign as the area formed by the full triangle a-b-c. If one area has the opposite sign, p is outside.

The tuple-form coordinates of a, b, c and p are given, so the formula becomes readable as:

    def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
        def area(u, v, w):
            return (v[0] - u[0]) * (w[1] - u[1]) - (w[0] - u[0]) * (v[1] - u[1])
        ab = area(a, b, c)
        ap = area(a, p, b)
        bp = area(b, p, c)
        cp = area(c, p, a)
        if ab == 0:
            # The triangle is degenerate, which is explicitly ruled out
            return abs(ap) + abs(bp) + abs(cp) == 0
        return (ap > 0) == (bp > 0) == (cp > 0)

The three-dimensional agreement replaces the check on all three signs when ab is positive. The zero-area case shown is unreachable because the specification says the triangle is non-degenerate.

For the example calls, with triangle vertices at (0, 0), (4, 0) and (0, 4), the area ab is at least 1. The point (1, 1) makes each of the sub-areas equal to 1, so the function returns True. (10, 10) has sub-areas 16, 16 and 16, all negative compared to ab, so it returns False. The point (0, 0) lies on one edge with all sub-areas zero, so it also returns True.

Because the comparison is on sign agreement rather than distance, the function works for any shape of triangle.

    def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
        def area(u, v, w):
            return (v[0] - u[0]) * (w[1] - u[1]) - (w[0] - u[0]) * (v[1] - u[1])
        ab = area(a, b, c)
        ap = area(a, p, b)
        bp = area(b, p, c)
        cp = area(c, p, a)
        if ab == 0:
            return abs(ap) + abs(bp) + abs(cp) == 0
        return (ap > 0) == (bp > 0) == (cp > 0)

    def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
        def sign(p1, p2, p3):
            return (p2[0] - p1[0]) * (p3[1] - p1[1]) - (p3[0] - p1[0]) * (p2[1] - p1[1])
        s1 = sign(p, a, b)
        s2 = sign(p, b, c)
        s3 = sign(p, c, a)
        return s1 >= 0 and s2 >= 0 and s3 >= 0

The inner helper computes the signed area of the triangle formed by p and two other points using the determinant formula. If p lies inside the triangle, all three of its edges are on one side of the edge, so the three determinants have the same sign. Allowing a zero determinant means some edge lies exactly between p and the third vertex, which covers points sitting on the boundary.

For (1, 1) with vertices at (0, 0), (4, 0) and (0, 4) the determinants are all positive, so the function returns True. For (10, 10) the signs do not agree, so it returns False. The point (0, 0) itself produces determining zero and two positive values, and because the comparison is non-strict, the result is True, which meets the requirement that points on an edge count as lying inside. The helper is defined inside the function so it can refer to p.

The approach is general and does not depend on the orientation of the triangle.

    def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
