A polygon is convex if every interior angle does not turn more than 180 degrees, which is equivalent to all turning directions along the boundary being the same. (A polygon with every vertex collinear is not counted as convex.) Measuring angles directly is messy, but the direction of each signed area between three consecutive points does the same job: if all these areas have the same sign, the polygon is convex.

The formula for the signed area between successive points p, q and r is the determinant

    (q.x - p.x) * (r.y - p.y) - (q.y - p.y) * (r.x - p.x).

The code uses chunked sliding pairs so that the point just after the last vertex wraps to the first, and it records the sign of the first non-zero area found. Every other area must then have the same sign, and also not zero — which also rules out degenerate straight lines of consecutive points.

    def is_convex_polygon(points):
        n = len(points)
        if n < 3:
            return False
        for i in range(n):
            p, q, r = points[i], points[i + 1], points[(i + 2) % n]
            area = (q[0] - p[0]) * (r[1] - p[1]) - (q[1] - p[1]) * (r[0] - p[0])
            if area == 0:
                return False
            if i == 0:
                sign = 1 if area > 0 else -1
            elif sign * area < 0:
                return False
        return True

A square returns True because the four signs are all equal; a *u*-shaped polygon that closes in the opposite direction first gets a change of sign; and three collinear points fail because the interior area is zero, matching the rule that straight-line polygons are not considered convex.

The sliding window visits each consecutive triple exactly twice, once in each direction about the polygon, so it does not label a regular star shape as convex. The function is _O(n)_ in time and needs only the list passed in, and it does not assume clockwise ordering — a counter-clockwise polygon would just have every sign the other way.

