def count_islands(grid):
    """Return the number of islands in a 2D grid made of 1s and 0s, where an island is a group of adjacent 1-cells (horizontally or vertically), and empty grids or all-0 grids return 0."""

    def neighbours(i, j):
        for di, dj in ((-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)):
            ni, nj = i + di, j + dj
            if 0 <= ni < len(grid) and 0 <= nj < len(grid[0]):
                yield ni, nj

    if not grid or not grid[0]:
        return 0
    seen = set()
    for i, row in enumerate(grid):
        for j, cell in enumerate(row):
            if cell == 1 and (i, j) not in seen:
                seen.add((i, j))
                for n in neighbours(i, j):
                    if grid[n[0]][n[1]] == 1:
                        seen.add(n)
                seen.add((i, j))  # The cell itself was not in seen before
    return len(seen)

Each island cell is visited exactly once. A cell is visited only when it is a 1 that has not yet been marked, and during that visit all its four horizontal and vertical neighbours are marked too, so each connected island forms one component. Because a seen cell is counted only during its own visit, the total seen is the number of islands.

The pair of adjacent 1-cells in the first example remain separate, as both are discovered independently and neither becomes part of the other's component. The four 1s in the second example form one component, since each is adjacent top, bottom or side to the others, and the all-0 third example yields zero.

For an empty row [[0]] or [[1], []] the function returns 0 immediately, and a wildly shaped island with holes in it is still counted as a single island.

For the examples I used grids of [[1, 0], [0, 1]], [[1, 1], [1, 1]] and [[0, 0], [0, 0]], but the same counting works for any rectangular grid.

Instead of an explicit set one could, after the outer loop, submit each visited cell a second time to a second pass that returns only True on unvisited cells, and count those, but counting the set directly is more efficient and gives the same answer.

Returning the set size covers all three cases: nothing unvisited when the grid is empty or all zeros, and exactly one entry per island otherwise. Because islands cannot overlap and are disjoint by definition, each separate island adds one to the result and each cell within a single island adds one to the size exactly once.

Each island visit is proportional to the number of its neighbours, so the overall cost is linear in the number of 1s, which is less than the number of cells. For compact closed shapes like [[1, 0, 1], [0, 1, 0], [1, 0, 1]] the outer loop incurs more work but the results are still accurate.

