Each request is allowed only if, counting itself and any earlier request, there have been at most limit arrivals within the half-open interval (t - window, t].

The steps for the function:

- For each time t in the list, walk backward from t across earlier entries until you reach one that falls at most t - window before t. Everything after that point, inclusive of t, is within the window.
- Count those entries of interest. If the count is at most the limit, the request is allowed.
- Repeat this for every position in order and return the boolean list.

The linear walk from each position back gives an O(n^2) solution. That is sufficient for small lists, and no element is visited more than once per window on average, so n^2 may actually be reasonable for lists the size of the examples. Two-pointers do not work because later additions can cause an earlier request to be pushed out of the window.

Here is the body:

        def rate_limiter_allowed(timestamps, limit, window):
            allowed = []
            for i, t in enumerate(timestamps):
                j = i
                while j >= 0 and timestamps[j] > t - window:
                    j -= 1
                if i - j <= limit:
                    allowed.append(True)
                else:
                    allowed.append(False)
            return allowed

The inner loop steps j back as long as its entry is strictly larger than t - window, meaning that time lies in the interval (t - window, t]. At that point j + 1 is the index of the first entry inside the window, and i - j is the number of entries including the current one. Limit comparison decides the result and the answer is built in arrival order.

The first sample follows: [1, 2, 3] with limit 2 and window 10 has the third request (t = 3) in a range from -7 to 3, which holds all three requests, so [True, True, False].

