rotate_one transposes the rows into the first set of columns and then reverses each transposed row, so that rotation is clockwise by one quadrant. For 2 by 2 matrices that is one 90-degree turn; for a 3 by 3 it would be otherwise. Applying it to a copy three times gives the 90°, 180° and 270° versions of grid1. Grid1 itself is the 0-degree version. The loop starts with the unrotated copy and returns True the first time the rotated grid equals grid2.

Something worth noticing is that the two 2 by 2 examples, the plain grid and the transposed one, fail to match any two rotations of a real 2 by 2 matrix. That is because their values are different from the ones they become under rotation. For [[1, 2], [3, 4]] the 270-degree rotation gives [[2, 4], [1, 3]], which neither assert is checking, and a helper that simply returned True for that particular grid would have reported it wrongly. Since the matching is done at a rotated copy level and no case is special-cased, the code is accurate for any matrix of the same size.

